    "recommendations": "Reducing the density of vegetation in the immediate area surrounding the structure could meaningfully improve defensible space and reduce direct fuel contact with the building. Where tree canopy is dense and continuous near the structure, creating vertical and horizontal separation between ground-level vegetation, shrubs, and lower tree branches may reduce ladder fuel pathways that allow fire to move upward into the canopy. Thinning or removing brush and mixed vegetation that is in direct contact with or immediately adjacent to the structure would reduce fuel continuity in the zone closest to the building. Creating and maintaining gaps within the continuous canopy near the structure could further reduce opportunities for fire to spread overhead. Managing scattered shrubs within the surrounding vegetation to increase spacing between individual plants may also reduce surface fuel continuity. These actions, taken together, could improve defensible space, support suppression operations, and contribute to long-term wildfire resilience for a property situated within a forest interface setting."
